  "Name": "Shen2009-Cr-m1-4-m2-4",
  "Source": "W. Shen, X. Sun, Y. Zhang, Z. Luo, X. Liu, and P. Gu, \"Narrow band filters in both transmission and reflection with metal/dielectric thin films,\" Opt. Commun. 282, 242-246 (2009). doi:10.1016/j.optcom.2008.09.080 - Sec. 2 pp. 244-245 and Fig. 4 (m1 = 4, m2 = 4); same materials and Cr as Sec. 2 p. 243",
  "Notes": "Metal/dielectric narrow-band filter peaking in both reflection and transmission at 700 nm (m1 = m2 = 4, symmetric mirrors, maximum Tmax)\nSpecification: Reflectance and transmittance both maximal at 700 nm; with m1 = m2 the two cavity mirrors are closest in reflectance, giving the highest Tmax.\nReported: Calculated (Fig. 4, right column): Rmax = 32.8 % at 700 nm; Tmax = 44.1 % at 700 nm; R ~0 near 670 and 715 nm, rising to ~7-9 % at 640 and 760 nm. Measured (Fig. 5): Rmax = 34 %, Tmax = 45.2 % near 698 nm, transmittance FWHM 7.7 nm.\nNotes: Stack written from the incident side as in the paper (Air/Cr/(LH)^4/2L/(HL)^4/H/Sub): 1 + 8 + 1 + 8 + 1 = 19 layers. Same assumptions as Shen2009-Cr-m1-2-m2-4: quarter waves at 700 nm, constant nH = 2.17, nL = 1.45, ns = 1.52 with back surface ignored, 5 nm Cr from Table 1 (no 700 nm entry, nearest 704 nm), normal incidence.\nLayers from Sec. 2 pp. 244-245 and Fig. 4 (m1 = 4, m2 = 4); same materials and Cr as Sec. 2 p. 243; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours R max 36.4 % at 699.9 nm, T max 49.0 %; paper calculated R max 32.8 %, T max 44.1 % at 700 nm. Ours is 4-5 points higher; the paper’s Cr table has no point between 653 and 704 nm, so the Cr index at 700 nm is interpolated here and the paper may have used a different value.",
